Fish: state properties that Fish blocks add to their template's (P8.3).

A content mod's block copies its template's states one to one. A Fish block may declare state
properties of its own (`state_properties` in `data/<ns>/horizon/block/<path>.json`: a name, every
value in order and the default). Its states are then the template's, each once per combination of
the added values, in vanilla's order, which `StateTable` checks: every property sorted by name, the
last one changing fastest (`state_layout` orders the (template state, combination) pairs). Every
per-state table of the overlay gets them:

- horizon-mods `content.rs`: the `StateProperty` declaration and `Block::state_properties`;
- `mod_content.rs`: `Rename` maps a template state and a combination to its place, `states_of`
  emits every state and writes the added properties into the `facts.json` rows (state and
  description), the NBT tables' `states` lists get them too and `cached_shapes` follows;
  `model-states.json` gets the added schema and the combined possible states,
  `render-definitions.json` every id and the default; the block's default state has every added
  property at its default.

A name the template already uses, fewer than two distinct values, a default that is not a value or
more than 4096 combinations refuse the content, as other declaration errors do. Without added
properties nothing changes.

